        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Technology for controlling the pressure within the eye is currently limited to ophthalmic drops, laser treatments, or surgical procedures including microinvasive glaucoma stents and invasive procedures that create an alternative drainage pathway in the eye. None of these methods can predict how much, if any, the pressure within the eye may be changed. The Balance Goggles function by simply adding vacuum or pressure around the orbit of the eye, resulting in a normalization of the pressure differential in optic nerve diseases such as glaucoma, idiopathic intracranial hypertension (IIH), hypotony, and visual impairment and intraocular pressure (VIIP).
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
The purpose of this study is to confirm the safety and efficacy of the Balance Goggles on humans when subjected to a change in pressure of the microenvironment of the eye. Investigators will measure the safety of the Balance Goggles by measuring intraocular pressure (IOP), tear break up time (TBUT), refraction, keratometry, and performing a peripheral retinal exam both prior to pressure changes and after pressure changes. They will also measure optical coherence tomography (OCT) of the optic nerve prior, during, and after administering the change in orbital pressure to determine the ability of the device to change the morphology of the optic nerve head. Additionally they will assess qualitative measures such as comfort prior, during, and after administration of the pressure change treatments.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
Results for this study aren’t available at this time. 
<p>
